gah, du'u and sedu'u so are so closely related that it's hard to distinguish
them.  So, {se du'u} is a piece of text that expresses a {du'u} which is a
fact.  I've been using {cusku lo du'u} all this time when I really meant {se
du'u}.  {mi djuno lo du'u broda}  but {mi cusku lo se du'u do broda}.

On Fri, Oct 1, 2010 at 1:09 PM, Kevin Reid <kpreid@switchb.org> wrote:

>
> On Oct 1, 2010, at 11:58, Luke Bergen wrote:
>
>  oops.  I thought that's what {du'u} was.  I thought {se du'u} was "the
>> sentence that expresses a bridi".  So I would think that {se du'u} is the
>> actual sentence and {du'u} is the basic relationship that the {se du'u} is
>> expressing.
>>
>
> Yes, exactly so.
>
> 1. You said "you are fat" to me    do cusku lu do plana li'u mi
> 2. You said that I am fat          do cusku lo se du'u mi plana
>
> #1 implies #2; #2 is less specific than #1, in that it does not give exact
> phrasing.
>
> Why do you think you need to use some other abstraction than {du'u}?
>
>
